Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedbool.shoddy — reckoner seed: bool.shoddy's binary layer
Rem
Rem HEX, BIN, INBASE, FROMBASE; BITAND, BITOR, BITXOR, BITNOT, SHL, SHR;
Rem MASK, GETFIELD, BITAT, POPCOUNT; and the six logic gates the engine
Rem core does not already own — NAND, NOR, XOR, XNOR, IMPLIES, IFF.
Rem RckSeedBool(st) is the whole of this file's public surface: fold it
Rem over a state and all twenty are in the dictionary.
Rem
Rem THE WIDEST SEED, because bool is the widest domain bridged: bases,
Rem bit operations, masks and gates are four families that share nothing
Rem but the value they work on, and a keyboard that has one of them and
Rem not the others is a keyboard that cannot finish the job.
Rem
Rem EVERY BOOL* WORD ABORTS ON A BAD DOMAIN AND NOTHING KEYABLE MAY.
Rem That is the whole work of this file. bool.shoddy is deliberately
Rem strict — it refuses a negative, a fraction, a value that does not fit
Rem its width, a width outside 1..53 — and every one of those refusals is
Rem an Error, which would take the mill down with it. So each word below
Rem asks the question bool would abort on FIRST, and answers a refusal
Rem instead. R4.1, applied to a machine that guards itself thoroughly and
Rem in the one way a reckoner cannot use.
Rem
Rem The guard helpers answer a REASON STRING, empty when there is
Rem nothing wrong. A Result would carry a payload none of them has: the
Rem question is only ever "may this proceed, and if not why not".
Rem
Rem NOT BRIDGED, each for its own reason:
Rem
Rem   * AND, OR, NOT. The engine core owns all three as Boolean words
Rem     already. Bridging bool's would give two spellings for one idea,
Rem     which is the rule seed-math applied to Rad and Deg. The six gates
Rem     that ARE here are the ones the core lacks — and they have to be
Rem     here, because A USER WORD TAKES EXACTLY ONE CELL, so a keyboard
Rem     user cannot define a two-argument XOR for themselves.
Rem
Rem   * Truth tables and minimisation. BoolTruthTable answers a
Rem     List Of BoolRow and BoolMinimise a List Of BoolTerm, and neither
Rem     is a Cell — cuttle's value set has no case for either. Bridging
Rem     them means inventing a stack representation for a product term,
Rem     which is a design and not a bridge.
Rem
Rem   * BoolLowBit and BoolHighBit. They answer an Option, and None() at
Rem     zero is an ORDINARY answer that a stack has nowhere to put. A
Rem     word that refused on zero would contradict the contract bool
Rem     itself draws, and POPCOUNT and BITAT answer the questions a
Rem     keyboard actually asks.
Rem
Rem   * BoolCard. Its glyphs are UTF-8 and file I/O is Latin-1, so a card
Rem     that reaches a tape or a saved session is a row of question
Rem     marks. BIN is the render that survives being written down.
Rem
Rem   * The rotates, BoolShrA, the two's-complement family and the codes.
Rem     Bridgeable, and deliberately left: each wants a width the
Rem     keyboard must keep supplying, and a seed of forty words has
Rem     stopped being a bridge and become a second dictionary. A mill
Rem     that wants them registers them itself — RckReg is public, and
Rem     that is the whole point of the seed pattern.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "bool.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- pulling several whole numbers at once ----------------------------
Rem RckWholeArg already refuses a negative and a fraction, which is two
Rem of bool's three domain rules; the third is the 53-bit ceiling, and it
Rem is checked here because a value above it is no longer exactly itself
Rem and every answer built from it would be plausible and wrong.

Def BlWhole(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckWholeArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(x)
            BlUnderCeiling(word, x)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def BlUnderCeiling(word As String, x As Number) As Result
    If x > 2 ^ BoolMaxWidth() - 1 Then
        Err(word & " needs a whole number below 2^" & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(x), 0)
    Else
        Ok(x)

Rem Deepest first, stopping at the first argument that is not a whole
Rem number — seed-math's MNums, over bool's stricter reader.
Def BlWholes(word As String, vs As List Of Cell) As Result
    BlWholesAt(word, vs, 1, { })

Def BlWholesAt(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, k As Number, acc As List Of Number) As Result
    If k > Length(vs) Then
        Ok(acc)
    Else
        BlWholesNext(word, vs, k, acc, BlWhole(word, Nth(vs, k)))

Def BlWholesNext(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, k As Number, acc As List Of Number, r As Result) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(x)
            BlWholesAt(word, vs, k + 1, Append(acc, x))
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Rem ---- the reason strings ------------------------------------------------
Rem Empty means "nothing wrong, go ahead". Each names the bound it is
Rem enforcing rather than only that something was out of range, because
Rem "1..53" is the fact a caller needs and "bad width" is not.

Def BlWidthWhy(word As String, a As Number, w As Number) As String
    If w < 1 Or w > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a width from 1 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(w)
    Else
        If a >= 2 ^ w Then
            word & " needs a value that fits " & Str(w) & " bits, and " & Str(a) & " does not"
        Else
            ""

Def BlBaseWhy(word As String, b As Number) As String
    If b < 2 Or b > 36 Then
        word & " needs a base from 2 to 36, got " & Str(b)
    Else
        ""

Def BlIdxWhy(word As String, k As Number) As String
    If k > BoolMaxWidth() - 1 Then
        word & " needs a bit index from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth() - 1) & ", got " & Str(k)
    Else
        ""

Def BlCountWhy(word As String, n As Number) As String
    If n > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a bit count from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(n)
    Else
        ""

Def BlFieldWhy(word As String, hi As Number, lo As Number) As String
    If BlIdxWhy(word, hi) <> "" Then
        BlIdxWhy(word, hi)
    Else
        If BlIdxWhy(word, lo) <> "" Then
            BlIdxWhy(word, lo)
        Else
            If hi < lo Then
                word & " needs a high bit at or above the low bit, got " & Str(hi) & " and " & Str(lo)
            Else
                ""

Def BlShiftWhy(word As String, n As Number) As String
    If n > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a shift from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(n)
    Else
        ""

Rem EVERY WORD BELOW TESTS ITS REASON WITH If/Then/Else AND NEVER Ifte.
Rem Ifte is an ordinary call, so both of its arms are evaluated before it
Rem chooses between them — handing it a bool word as one arm would run
Rem exactly the abort the guard was written to prevent. If/Then/Else
Rem compiles to two quotations and one Ifte over them, so only the chosen
Rem arm runs. This is the single most dangerous line to "tidy up" in the
Rem file.

Rem ---- HEX, BIN ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Total once the domain is checked: every whole number below the ceiling
Rem has a rendering in every base.

Def BlSeedHex(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "HEX", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlText("HEX", First(vs), BoolHex)), "( n -- s )", "n written in hexadecimal, uppercase.")

Def BlSeedBin(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BIN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlText("BIN", First(vs), BoolBinS)), "( n -- s )", "n written in binary.")

Def BlText(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole(word, c)
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, n)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- INBASE, FROMBASE --------------------------------------------------
Rem The general pair. FROMBASE reads text, so a bad digit is a fact about
Rem the input rather than a bug: bool answers Result there and this turns
Rem that into a refusal carrying the position, in seed-csv's shape.

Def BlSeedInbase(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "INBASE", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlInbase(vs)), "( n base -- s )", "n written in the given base, 2 to 36, uppercase letters past 9.")

Def BlInbase(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("INBASE", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlInbaseAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("INBASE did not answer")

Def BlInbaseAt(n As Number, b As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlBaseWhy("INBASE", b)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutStr(BoolInBase(n, b)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedFrombase(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "FROMBASE", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlFrombase(vs)), "( s base -- n )", "The digit string s read in the given base; refuses a digit the base does not have, naming its position.")

Def BlFrombase(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("FROMBASE", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(s)
            BlFrombaseAt(s, Nth(vs, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Def BlFrombaseAt(s As String, c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("FROMBASE", c)
        Case Ok(b)
            BlFrombaseOn(s, b)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Def BlFrombaseOn(s As String, b As Number) As RckWork
    If BlBaseWhy("FROMBASE", b) <> "" Then
        RckWorkBad(BlBaseWhy("FROMBASE", b))
    Else
        BlFrombaseRead(BoolFromBase(s, b))

Def BlFrombaseRead(r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutNum(n) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(BlWhyAt("FROMBASE", w, at))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Rem bool's own messages open with the BOOL* word that raised them, and at
Rem the keyboard the word is FROMBASE. So the prefix up to the first colon
Rem is REPLACED rather than repeated — "FROMBASE could not read it:
Rem BOOLFROMBASE: ..." names two words for one refusal and neither of them
Rem clearly. The rest of the message is bool's verbatim, because it is the
Rem only place that knows which of the three failures it was: a digit the
Rem base does not have, an empty string, or a string too long to read
Rem exactly. Err carries no tag to tell them apart by.
Def BlReword(word As String, why As String) As String
    Let k = Instr(why, ": ")
    If k = 0 Then
        word & ": " & why
    Else
        word & Mid(why, k, Len(why))

Def BlWhyAt(word As String, why As String, at As Number) As String
    If at = 0 Then
        BlReword(word, why)
    Else
        BlReword(word, why) & " at position " & Str(at)

Rem ---- BITAND, BITOR, BITXOR ---------------------------------------------
Rem The three width-free words, and the only three that need no bound
Rem beyond the domain itself.

Def BlSeedBitand(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITAND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITAND", vs, BoolBitAnd)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise AND. Needs no width.")

Def BlSeedBitor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITOR", vs, BoolBitOr)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise OR. Needs no width.")

Def BlSeedBitxor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITXOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITXOR", vs, BoolBitXor)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise XOR. Needs no width.")

Def BlBits2(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- BITNOT ------------------------------------------------------------
Rem Takes a width, because a complement without one has no answer.

Def BlSeedBitnot(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITNOT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitnot(vs)), "( a width -- c )", "Bitwise NOT within the given width, 1 to 53.")

Def BlBitnot(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("BITNOT", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlBitnotAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BITNOT did not answer")

Def BlBitnotAt(a As Number, w As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlWidthWhy("BITNOT", a, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolBitNot(a, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem ---- SHL, SHR ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem SHL takes a width and SHR does not, which is bool's own rule carried
Rem to the keyboard: a logical right shift cannot produce a bit the input
Rem did not have, so there is no end for its answer to depend on.

Def BlSeedShl(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SHL", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlShl(vs)), "( a n width -- c )", "a shifted left n places within the given width; bits above it fall off.")

Def BlShl(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("SHL", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlShlAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), Nth(ns, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SHL did not answer")

Def BlShlAt(a As Number, n As Number, w As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlShlWhy(a, n, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolShl(a, n, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlShlWhy(a As Number, n As Number, w As Number) As String
    If BlShiftWhy("SHL", n) <> "" Then
        BlShiftWhy("SHL", n)
    Else
        BlWidthWhy("SHL", a, w)

Def BlSeedShr(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SHR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlShr(vs)), "( a n -- c )", "a shifted right n places, zeros coming in at the top. Needs no width.")

Def BlShr(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("SHR", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlShrAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SHR did not answer")

Def BlShrAt(a As Number, n As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlShiftWhy("SHR", n)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolShr(a, n)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem ---- MASK, GETFIELD, BITAT, POPCOUNT -----------------------------------

Def BlSeedMask(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "MASK", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlMask(First(vs))), "( n -- m )", "A mask with the low n bits set, n from 0 to 53.")

Def BlMask(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("MASK", c)
        Case Ok(n)
            BlMaskAt(n)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("MASK did not answer")

Def BlMaskAt(n As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlCountWhy("MASK", n)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolMask(n)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedGetfield(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "GETFIELD", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlGetfield(vs)), "( a hi lo -- v )", "Bits hi down to lo of a, inclusive and 0-based, shifted down to zero.")

Def BlGetfield(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("GETFIELD", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlGetfieldAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), Nth(ns, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("GETFIELD did not answer")

Def BlGetfieldAt(a As Number, hi As Number, lo As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlFieldWhy("GETFIELD", hi, lo)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolGetField(a, hi, lo)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedBitat(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITAT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitat(vs)), "( a k -- flag )", "Whether bit k of a is set. Answers a BOOLEAN, never 1 or 0.")

Def BlBitat(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("BITAT", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlBitatAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BITAT did not answer")

Def BlBitatAt(a As Number, k As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlIdxWhy("BITAT", k)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutBool(BoolBitAt(a, k)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedPopcount(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "POPCOUNT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlPopcount(First(vs))), "( a -- n )", "How many of a's bits are set.")

Def BlPopcount(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("POPCOUNT", c)
        Case Ok(a)
            RckVals({ CutNum(BoolPopCount(a)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("POPCOUNT did not answer")

Rem ---- the six gates the core does not own -------------------------------
Rem RckBool2 reads both arguments as BOOLEANs and names the kind it got
Rem instead, so these are one line each. AND, OR and NOT are the engine
Rem core's and are not redefined here.

Def BlSeedNand(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "NAND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("NAND", vs, BoolNand)), "( p q -- r )", "NOT (p AND q).")

Def BlSeedNor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "NOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("NOR", vs, BoolNor)), "( p q -- r )", "NOT (p OR q).")

Def BlSeedXor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "XOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("XOR", vs, BoolXor)), "( p q -- r )", "True when exactly one of p and q is true.")

Def BlSeedXnor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "XNOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("XNOR", vs, BoolXnor)), "( p q -- r )", "True when p and q agree.")

Def BlSeedImplies(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "IMPLIES", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("IMPLIES", vs, BoolImplies)), "( p q -- r )", "False on exactly one row: p true and q false.")

Def BlSeedIff(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "IFF", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("IFF", vs, BoolIff)), "( p q -- r )", "Material equivalence — the same answer as XNOR, under a logician's name.")

Rem ---- the seed -----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedBool(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let st1 = BlSeedHex(RckSeeding(st, "seed-bool"))
    Let st2 = BlSeedBin(st1)
    Let st3 = BlSeedInbase(st2)
    Let st4 = BlSeedFrombase(st3)
    Let st5 = BlSeedBitand(st4)
    Let st6 = BlSeedBitor(st5)
    Let st7 = BlSeedBitxor(st6)
    Let st8 = BlSeedBitnot(st7)
    Let st9 = BlSeedShl(st8)
    Let st10 = BlSeedShr(st9)
    Let st11 = BlSeedMask(st10)
    Let st12 = BlSeedGetfield(st11)
    Let st13 = BlSeedBitat(st12)
    Let st14 = BlSeedPopcount(st13)
    Let st15 = BlSeedNand(st14)
    Let st16 = BlSeedNor(st15)
    Let st17 = BlSeedXor(st16)
    Let st18 = BlSeedXnor(st17)
    Let st19 = BlSeedImplies(st18)
    BlSeedIff(st19)
